WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.778
Here's a line from mathematics.

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:04.900
x = x + 1.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.288
As a statement in maths, it's simply false.

00:00:08.288 --> 00:00:10.690
No number is one more than itself.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.109
Now here's the same line in Python.

00:00:13.109 --> 00:00:16.360
Programmers write it every day, and it works perfectly.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.910
So what does the equals sign mean in Python, if it doesn't mean equal?

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.238
To answer that, we need a picture of what Python keeps in memory.

00:00:26.238 --> 00:00:29.260
By the end of this video, you'll be able to draw it.

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:36.153
Welcome to Lecture 2 of Introduction to AI Programming: Python basics.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:40.604
In this first part, we'll talk to Python in the shell and in scripts,

00:00:40.604 --> 00:00:44.823
see what an assignment really does, and learn how to choose good names.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.130
Python comes with an interactive shell.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:54.680
You type one line after the three arrows, called the prompt, press Enter, and Python answers at once.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.746
Print is a function: a named piece of code that does a job.

00:00:58.746 --> 00:01:04.350
You call it by writing its name, then parentheses, with what it should print inside them.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.500
The text in quotes, 'hello world', is called a string.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.671
You have to say something Python understands.

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:14.994
Type an English question, and you get a syntax error.

00:01:14.994 --> 00:01:17.250
Python doesn't guess what you meant.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.479
That's not a judgement on you.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:23.738
Python knows its own language exactly, and you're still learning it.

00:01:23.738 --> 00:01:28.560
It's far easier for you to learn Python than for a computer to learn English.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:32.910
When you're done, type exit(), to leave the shell.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.968
The shell is fine for experiments of 3 or 4 lines.

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:42.980
Real programs are longer, so we type them into a file, and ask Python to run the whole file.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:47.271
That file is called a script, or a program.

00:01:47.271 --> 00:01:50.570
By convention, its name ends in .py.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.978
Here's a short script.

00:01:52.978 --> 00:01:55.130
Line 3 works out x + y.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:56.720
Line 4 prints it.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:00.086
Run it, and you see 30, just once.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:06.916
In a script, an expression on its own line is computed, and then thrown away.

00:02:06.916 --> 00:02:09.310
Only print produces output.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.457
Type the same lines into the shell, and you see 30 twice.

00:02:13.457 --> 00:02:18.870
The shell echoes the value of every expression you type, and then print shows it again.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:24.900
So if your script runs but shows nothing, check that you printed what you wanted to see.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:28.867
Learning Python is like learning any language.

00:02:29.217 --> 00:02:36.817
First, vocabulary: the names you make up, and the reserved words that Python already uses.

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:42.847
Second, sentence structure: the patterns Python accepts, called syntax.

00:02:43.197 --> 00:02:49.517
Third, story structure: sentences put together into a program that does something useful.

00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:57.307
This lecture covers the vocabulary, and the first kinds of sentence: assignments, expressions, and print.

00:02:58.101 --> 00:03:01.044
Here's our first sentence: x is bound to 10.

00:03:01.044 --> 00:03:06.061
It's called an assignment statement, and it does two things, in this order.

00:03:06.411 --> 00:03:13.721
First, Python evaluates the right-hand side, and gets an object: a value in memory.

00:03:13.721 --> 00:03:15.571
Here, the integer 10.

00:03:16.171 --> 00:03:21.623
Second, Python binds the name on the left, x, to that object.

00:03:21.623 --> 00:03:24.931
From now on, the name x refers to it.

00:03:25.531 --> 00:03:29.959
A name together with the object it refers to is called a binding.

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:33.092
All the bindings of a program live in a frame.

00:03:33.092 --> 00:03:36.771
For the code in this lecture, that's the global frame.

00:03:37.121 --> 00:03:38.591
Now bind y to 20.

00:03:38.591 --> 00:03:43.521
Another name in the frame, another arrow, another object.

00:03:43.871 --> 00:03:48.096
A name that refers to an object is called a variable.

00:03:48.096 --> 00:03:52.799
Ask the shell for x, and it follows the arrow and shows 10.

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:55.191
Ask for x + y, and you get 30.

00:03:55.541 --> 00:04:03.850
This is the names and objects diagram: names on the left in the frame, objects on the right, and an arrow from each name to its object.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:08.221
It's the picture Python Tutor draws, and we'll use it in every lecture.

00:04:09.001 --> 00:04:11.490
Now let's assign to x again.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.001
x is bound to 20.

00:04:13.351 --> 00:04:16.368
This doesn't change the object 10.

00:04:16.368 --> 00:04:21.871
Python makes a new object, 20, and moves the arrow of x to it.

00:04:22.471 --> 00:04:27.071
Nothing refers to the 10 any more, so Python frees its memory.

00:04:27.421 --> 00:04:29.111
This is called rebinding.

00:04:29.111 --> 00:04:32.762
A name can be bound to a different object at any time.

00:04:32.762 --> 00:04:35.061
That's why it's called a variable.

00:04:35.411 --> 00:04:40.500
Your slides describe a variable as a named box that stores a value.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:46.652
That picture works at first, but it breaks as soon as two names share one object.

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:49.691
Arrows don't break, so we'll use arrows.

00:04:50.468 --> 00:04:53.348
Now we can answer the question from the start.

00:04:53.698 --> 00:04:55.491
Bind x to 2.

00:04:55.491 --> 00:04:58.778
Then, x becomes x + 2.

00:04:59.128 --> 00:05:02.998
This line is an instruction, not a fact.

00:05:02.998 --> 00:05:07.932
Evaluate x + 2, using the current x, which gives 4.

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:10.448
Then bind x to the result.

00:05:11.048 --> 00:05:13.688
So print x shows 4.

00:05:14.038 --> 00:05:19.861
Read the single equals sign as 'becomes', or 'is bound to'.

00:05:19.861 --> 00:05:21.638
Never as 'equals'.

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:24.558
The left side must be a name.

00:05:24.558 --> 00:05:31.118
Write 10 is bound to x, the other way round, and Python stops with a syntax error.

00:05:31.118 --> 00:05:33.038
You can't bind a number.

00:05:33.388 --> 00:05:35.601
Python does have a test for equality.

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:39.668
It's written with two equals signs, and you'll meet it in Lecture 3.

00:05:40.435 --> 00:05:41.771
Time to predict.

00:05:41.771 --> 00:05:43.107
Pause the video.

00:05:43.107 --> 00:05:47.115
Here are 4 lines. What does the last line print?

00:05:47.665 --> 00:05:48.983
Let's draw it.

00:05:48.983 --> 00:05:50.585
a is bound to 10.

00:05:50.935 --> 00:05:52.895
Then b is bound to a.

00:05:52.895 --> 00:06:00.268
Python evaluates a, which gives the object 10, and binds b to that same object.

00:06:00.268 --> 00:06:03.535
Now two arrows point at one object.

00:06:04.135 --> 00:06:06.592
Then a is bound to 20.

00:06:06.592 --> 00:06:10.055
That moves only the arrow of a.

00:06:10.405 --> 00:06:14.336
So the program prints 20, then 10.

00:06:14.336 --> 00:06:16.765
b still refers to 10.

00:06:17.365 --> 00:06:20.250
Assignment never links two names together.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.885
It only binds a name to whatever object the right-hand side gives, at that moment.

00:06:26.235 --> 00:06:31.129
If you pictured b as a box that holds a copy of a, or as tied to a,

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:35.000
that's the most common misconception in this lecture.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.995
Draw the arrows, and you won't be fooled.

00:06:38.768 --> 00:06:42.688
Variable names are up to you, within 4 rules.

00:06:43.038 --> 00:06:46.473
A name must start with a letter or an underscore.

00:06:46.473 --> 00:06:50.118
It may contain only letters, digits and underscores.

00:06:50.468 --> 00:06:52.077
Names are case sensitive.

00:06:52.077 --> 00:06:57.868
Apple with a small letter, with a capital, and in all capitals, are three different names.

00:06:58.218 --> 00:07:01.298
And a name must not be a reserved word.

00:07:01.648 --> 00:07:06.520
So apple, car, my_number_123, and _light are fine.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.150
The 4 on the right are not.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:16.848
One starts with a digit, and the others contain characters that aren't allowed.

00:07:17.198 --> 00:07:20.405
The reserved words are part of the language itself.

00:07:20.405 --> 00:07:23.865
There are 35 of them, and Python can list them for you.

00:07:23.865 --> 00:07:27.198
Try to use one as a name, and you get a syntax error.

00:07:27.968 --> 00:07:30.689
Legal isn't the same as good.

00:07:30.689 --> 00:07:33.128
What does this program do?

00:07:33.678 --> 00:07:38.359
Python doesn't care, and prints 437.5.

00:07:38.359 --> 00:07:41.438
But a reader has no idea.

00:07:41.788 --> 00:07:46.397
With meaningful names, the same program explains itself.

00:07:46.397 --> 00:07:48.948
Hours times rate gives the pay.

00:07:49.298 --> 00:07:54.457
Your slides suggest names in camel case, with a capital letter for each new word.

00:07:54.457 --> 00:08:00.444
Python's official style guide, called PEP 8, uses small letters joined by underscores instead.

00:08:00.444 --> 00:08:01.463
We follow PEP 8.

00:08:01.463 --> 00:08:02.418
Both are legal.

00:08:02.768 --> 00:08:09.648
And a name written in capitals, like seconds per minute, marks a constant that the program doesn't change.

00:08:10.435 --> 00:08:11.755
Let's sum up.

00:08:12.105 --> 00:08:18.265
The shell shows the value of every expression. A script only shows what it prints.

00:08:18.615 --> 00:08:25.017
An assignment evaluates the right side, then binds the name on the left to that object.

00:08:25.017 --> 00:08:26.415
Read it as becomes.

00:08:26.765 --> 00:08:32.285
Two names can share one object, and rebinding one of them never changes the other.

00:08:32.635 --> 00:08:33.787
Here's one to try.

00:08:33.787 --> 00:08:38.075
Draw the names and objects diagram after each line of this program,

00:08:38.075 --> 00:08:39.995
and write down what it prints.

00:08:40.545 --> 00:08:44.800
In part two, we'll look at the objects themselves: their types,

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.785
and how Python evaluates an expression, one step at a time.

